      "side": 1,
      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] In `get_gpiochip_by_dev`, there are a few issues with variable scoping, subprocess usage, and testability:\n\n1. **Global variable leak:** `chip` in `for chip in ...` is not declared `local`, leaking `chip` into the global shell namespace.\n2. **Unnecessary subprocesses \u0026 regex matching:** Piping `readlink \"${chip}\" | grep -q \"${match}\"` spawns a pipeline and `grep` process on every iteration and treats `match` as a regular expression rather than a literal substring; `[[ \"$(readlink \"${chip}\")\" \u003d\u003d *\"${match}\"* ]]` and `echo \"${chip##*/}\"` perform the check and basename extraction directly in Bash.\n3. **Testability seam:** Hardcoding `/sys/bus/gpio/devices` prevents unit-testing `get_gpiochip_by_dev` and `config_ocelot` in environments where `/sys/bus/gpio/devices/gpiochip*` is absent (where the unmatched glob stays a literal string). Allowing the directory to be overridden via `${SYSFS_GPIO_DEVICES_DIR:-/sys/bus/gpio/devices}` (similar to `lsbval` on line 477) enables hermetic unit testing with temporary symlinks.\n\n```bash\nget_gpiochip_by_dev() {\n  local match\u003d\"$1\"\n  local gpio_dev_dir\u003d\"${SYSFS_GPIO_DEVICES_DIR:-/sys/bus/gpio/devices}\"\n  local chip\n\n  for chip in \"${gpio_dev_dir}\"/gpiochip*; do\n    if [[ \"$(readlink \"${chip}\")\" \u003d\u003d *\"${match}\"* ]]; then\n      echo \"${chip##*/}\"\n      return 0\n    fi\n  done\n\n  return 1\n}\n```\n\n[🔗](http://go/tj/dce243a9-748e-41dd-ac66-ccb9c05317f1)",
