      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] When `GPIOSET_SUPPORTS_HOLD` is `1` (the default on ChromeOS), `_gpio_new` passes `${gpio_chip}` and `${gpio_line}` separately to `_gpioset_hold`. However, `_gpioset_hold` (lines 365–381) indexes `GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${signal}]` solely by the line offset (`$2`), and `GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS` is an undeclared indexed array.\n\nWith `\u003cchip\u003e:\u003cline\u003e` tuples across multiple GPIO controllers, two signals on different chips that share the same per-chip line number (e.g., `gpiochip0:15` and `gpiochip1:15`) will collide in `GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${signal}]`, causing `_gpioset_hold` to kill the `gpioset` background process holding the first chip\u0027s pin when setting the second.\n\nPlease declare `GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS` as an associative array at the top level (`declare -A GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS\u003d()`) and key it by `\"${gpio_chip}:${signal}\"` in `_gpioset_hold` (existing uses of `${#GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[@]}` on line 988 and `\"${GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[@]}\"` in the `EXIT` trap on line 1668 work unchanged with an associative array):\n\n```bash\ndeclare -A GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS\u003d()\n\n# Usage: _gpioset_hold \u003cgpio_chip\u003e \u003csignal\u003e \u003cvalue\u003e\n_gpioset_hold() {\n  local gpio_chip\u003d\"$1\"\n  local signal\u003d\"$2\"\n  local value\u003d\"$3\"\n  local key\u003d\"${gpio_chip}:${signal}\"\n\n  # Kill any gpioset processes for this GPIO before spawning a new one.\n  if [[ -n \"${GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${key}]}\" ]]; then\n    kill \"${GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${key}]}\"\n    unset \"GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${key}]\"\n  fi\n\n  # We need to run gpioset as a background process in order to hold the\n  # gpio at a specific value.\n  gpioset --mode\u003dsignal \"${gpio_chip}\" \"${signal}\u003d${value}\" \u0026\n  GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${key}]\u003d$!\n  klog \"Set and hold GPIO ${gpio_chip}/${signal} to ${value} (PID: ${GPIO_ACTIVE_PIDS[${key}]})\"\n}\n```\n\n[🔗](http://go/tj/dce243a9-748e-41dd-ac66-ccb9c05317f1)",
